WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.788
Schrödinger Bridges connect two probability
distributions with a diffusion process,

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:10.767
but nearly every existing solver is heavy: it
stacks several neural networks,

00:00:10.847 --> 00:00:14.906
needs adversarial min-max training, and can take
hours on a GPU.

00:00:14.986 --> 00:00:19.498
This paper introduces LightSB, a lightweight
Schrödinger Bridge solver.

00:00:19.578 --> 00:00:26.935
By parameterizing the Schrödinger potential as a
Gaussian mixture and treating its logarithm as an energy function,

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:29.651
LightSB turns the problem into one simple,

00:00:29.731 --> 00:00:33.725
non-minimax optimization objective with closed-form
components.

00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:38.964
The result solves Schrödinger Bridges in moderate
dimensions in minutes on a CPU,

00:00:39.044 --> 00:00:41.421
with no painful hyperparameter tuning,

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:45.560
and is provably a universal approximator of
Schrödinger Bridges.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.879
The Schrödinger Bridge problem asks for the
diffusion process between two given distributions

00:00:51.959 --> 00:00:55.891
that stays as close as possible to a reference
Wiener process.

00:00:55.971 --> 00:00:59.515
It is the dynamic version of entropic optimal transport,

00:00:59.595 --> 00:01:04.433
and it underpins applications from single-cell
biology to image translation.

00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:08.769
The trouble is that almost all existing solvers are
heavy-weighted.

00:01:08.849 --> 00:01:14.335
They parameterize the solution with several large
neural networks and require complex,

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:16.341
often adversarial optimization.

00:01:16.421 --> 00:01:20.159
As a result, the field lacks a simple, principled
baseline,

00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:26.760
the kind of go-to method that k-means is for
clustering or Sinkhorn is for discrete optimal transport.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:33.404
Consider a researcher who simply wants to compute
an entropic optimal transport or Schrödinger

00:01:33.484 --> 00:01:36.711
Bridge between two moderate-dimensional datasets.

00:01:36.791 --> 00:01:41.975
Today, that means adopting a solver with iterative
proportional fitting steps,

00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:43.595
or min-max optimization,

00:01:43.675 --> 00:01:48.184
or simulation of the full stochastic process at
every training step.

00:01:48.264 --> 00:01:53.178
These solvers demand careful neural network design,
run for hours on GPUs,

00:01:53.258 --> 00:01:56.012
and are sensitive to many hyperparameters.

00:01:56.092 --> 00:02:01.411
Yet in many real settings, the user only cares
about where the trajectories end,

00:02:01.491 --> 00:02:03.436
that is, the conditional plan.

00:02:03.516 --> 00:02:06.743
This mismatch, heavy machinery for a modest goal,

00:02:06.823 --> 00:02:09.780
is exactly the gap LightSB is built to close.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.812
The paper makes three contributions.

00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:17.543
First, it introduces LightSB,

00:02:17.623 --> 00:02:21.275
a lightweight solver that combines two recent
ideas: parameterizing the Schrödinger potential with sum-exp quadratic,

00:02:21.355 --> 00:02:25.007
that is Gaussian mixture, functions,

00:02:25.087 --> 00:02:28.738
and viewing the log-potential as an energy function.

00:02:28.818 --> 00:02:32.470
Together these yield a single, non-minimax,

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.202
simulation-free optimization objective with
closed-form expressions for the plan and the drift.

00:02:36.282 --> 00:02:39.933
Second,

00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:43.665
the authors prove that this Gaussian-mixture solver
is a universal approximator of Schrödinger Bridges,

00:02:43.745 --> 00:02:47.397
which they note is the first ever such result.

00:02:47.477 --> 00:02:51.128
Third,

00:02:51.208 --> 00:02:54.860
they analyze the generalization error and show it
converges at the standard parametric rate as sample size grows.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.427
LightSB starts from the goal of minimizing the
Kullback-Leibler divergence between the

00:02:59.507 --> 00:03:03.695
true entropic optimal transport plan and a
parameterized plan.

00:03:03.775 --> 00:03:07.962
The obstacle is that we do not know the true plan.

00:03:08.042 --> 00:03:12.229
The key move is to exploit the known form of that
plan and parameterize only the adjusted Schrödinger potential.

00:03:12.309 --> 00:03:16.497
The authors choose an unnormalized Gaussian mixture
for this potential.

00:03:16.577 --> 00:03:20.764
Under this choice,

00:03:20.844 --> 00:03:25.031
two things become tractable in closed form: the
conditional distributions of the plan,

00:03:25.111 --> 00:03:29.299
and the normalization constant.

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:33.566
Proposition 3.1 rewrites the KL objective as a
simple difference of two expectations,

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:37.833
of the log normalization under the source and the
log potential under the target,

00:03:37.913 --> 00:03:42.101
which can be estimated by Monte Carlo and optimized
with ordinary stochastic gradient descent.

00:03:42.181 --> 00:03:46.368
No min-max, no iterative proportional fitting,

00:03:46.448 --> 00:03:50.635
and no simulation of trajectories during training.

00:03:50.715 --> 00:03:54.903
Once trained, the same potential gives a
closed-form drift,

00:03:54.983 --> 00:03:59.170
so the full bridge process can be reconstructed directly.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:03.679
The solver is tested across four kinds of data.

00:04:03.759 --> 00:04:07.888
First, a two-dimensional Gaussian to Swiss-roll toy,

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:12.097
which visualizes how the noise level epsilon shapes
the trajectories.

00:04:12.177 --> 00:04:16.306
Second,

00:04:16.386 --> 00:04:20.515
a recent high-dimensional entropic optimal
transport benchmark with known ground-truth plans,

00:04:20.595 --> 00:04:24.725
spanning dimensions from two to one hundred
twenty-eight and several values of epsilon.

00:04:24.805 --> 00:04:28.934
Third, the MSCI single-cell dataset from a Kaggle
competition,

00:04:29.014 --> 00:04:33.143
with cells from four human donors at four time
points, projected by PCA to fifty,

00:04:33.223 --> 00:04:37.352
one hundred, and one thousand dimensions.

00:04:37.432 --> 00:04:41.561
And fourth, unpaired image translation on FFHQ faces,

00:04:41.641 --> 00:04:45.770
performed in the five-hundred-twelve-dimensional
latent space of a pretrained ALAE autoencoder.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.846
The results show both accuracy and speed.

00:04:48.926 --> 00:04:54.060
On the entropic optimal transport benchmark, where
the ground truth is known,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:59.613
LightSB reduces the conditional Bures-Wasserstein
error to well under one percent,

00:04:59.693 --> 00:05:01.644
often around a few hundredths,

00:05:01.724 --> 00:05:06.723
while the best previous solver sits between about
one and eighteen percent.

00:05:06.803 --> 00:05:08.686
On the MSCI single-cell task,

00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:13.223
LightSB reaches energy distance comparable to
strong GPU baselines,

00:05:13.303 --> 00:05:18.505
but it trains in roughly one to two and a half
minutes on just four CPU cores,

00:05:18.585 --> 00:05:24.463
whereas competing continuous solvers need tens of
minutes to over an hour on a V100 GPU.

00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:25.682
And on FFHQ faces,

00:05:25.762 --> 00:05:33.672
it performs realistic male-to-female and
child-to-adult translation in a five-hundred-twelve-dimensional latent space,

00:05:33.752 --> 00:05:36.110
converging in under a minute on CPU.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:40.636
To understand the role of the noise parameter epsilon,

00:05:40.716 --> 00:05:44.862
the authors map a two-dimensional Gaussian to a
Swiss roll while sweeping epsilon across three values.

00:05:44.942 --> 00:05:49.088
When epsilon is small,

00:05:49.168 --> 00:05:53.314
the learned process is nearly deterministic and its
trajectories are almost straight lines.

00:05:53.394 --> 00:05:57.540
As epsilon grows,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:01.766
the trajectories become more volatile and the
conditional distributions at the endpoint spread out.

00:06:01.846 --> 00:06:05.992
This matches the theory: epsilon controls the
stochasticity of the bridge.

00:06:06.072 --> 00:06:10.218
The benchmark results also reveal that LightSB
gains the most when the target distributions

00:06:10.298 --> 00:06:14.444
align with its Gaussian-mixture inductive bias,

00:06:14.524 --> 00:06:18.670
while remaining accurate across the tested
dimensions and noise levels.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.126
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:21.206 --> 00:06:27.331
LightSB converges in under one minute on four CPU
cores for the five-hundred-twelve-dimensional

00:06:27.411 --> 00:06:28.834
image translation task,

00:06:28.914 --> 00:06:30.075
with no GPU at all.

00:06:30.155 --> 00:06:33.994
On the hardest single-cell setting, one thousand
dimensions,

00:06:34.074 --> 00:06:40.069
it reaches an energy distance of one point two
seven in one hundred forty-six seconds on CPU,

00:06:40.149 --> 00:06:44.707
matching a minimax GPU solver that needs
seventy-one minutes on a V100.

00:06:44.787 --> 00:06:47.059
On the entropic transport benchmark,

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:51.893
its conditional error drops to as little as three
hundredths of a percent,

00:06:51.973 --> 00:06:56.596
against best-baseline errors ranging from about one
to eighteen percent.

00:06:56.676 --> 00:07:03.847
And all of this comes with a theoretical guarantee:
LightSB is a universal approximator of Schrödinger Bridges,

00:07:03.927 --> 00:07:08.550
with generalization error that vanishes at the
standard parametric rate.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:14.226
The lasting message is that a Schrödinger Bridge
solver does not have to be heavy.

00:07:14.306 --> 00:07:19.996
By parameterizing the transport potential as a
Gaussian mixture and optimizing a single,

00:07:20.076 --> 00:07:21.701
straightforward objective,

00:07:21.781 --> 00:07:27.339
LightSB solves entropic optimal transport and
Schrödinger Bridges in minutes on a CPU,

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:30.683
without adversarial training or process simulation.

00:07:30.763 --> 00:07:34.420
It matches or outperforms much heavier GPU-based solvers,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.813
comes with a universal-approximation guarantee, and
is easy to use.

00:07:38.893 --> 00:07:42.026
In short, LightSB is positioned to be the simple,

00:07:42.106 --> 00:07:46.550
reliable baseline that the Schrödinger Bridge field
has been missing.
